Subject: x87: Honor PC24/PC53 arithmetic and floating-store rounding.

Based on athei/x87sidecar 0b7c5e54dad37f6ed8374b91443e2d2083652452 (ported from 4e9c738).
TMNF uses control word 0x007f. Always computing in f64 ignores its PC field.

Share arithmetic emission across IR, direct, and peephole paths. Native f32
is used only with exact in-range operands, nearest host rounding, and a
normal f32 result. Other PC24 cases retain the f64 exponent range and round
the significand explicitly. Round-to-odd prevents intermediate double
rounding in nearest mode. Directed modes compose the requested rounding.
Preserve host FPCR, sticky FPSR flags, guest NZCV, and borrowed registers.
Update register-pressure estimates for the new temporary requirements.
FSqrt needs two helper FPRs; binary arithmetic needs three. Model one dying
input reused by these operations, including identical binary inputs.
Fix FXCH-prefixed fusions counting the absorbed instruction twice, expiring
live stack state early after pressure splits. Restore the delegated emitter's
budget before the dispatcher applies its ticks.

Add exact-rational arithmetic vectors, precision transitions, SSE state,
and optional PC24 coverage for integer arithmetic, flags and signal tests.
FMA contraction remains an explicitly unsafe upstream opt-in and should
remain disabled for TMNF, as must fast-round.

PC53 and all FST/FSTP m32 paths use guest RC with a scoped FPCR. Host FPCR
is restored exactly, FPSR retains old and new sticky flags, and NZCV is
untouched. Host flush/default-NaN/trap settings do not govern these operations.
This preserves the upstream sticky-host-flags contract, not full x87 SW
exception reporting or unmasked traps. PC64 still uses upstream's f64
approximation. Full80 exponent handling and PC24 extreme underflow are not
addressed here. Store rounding is independent of the precision-control field.

Port notes for 0b7c5e5: upstream now keeps x87 registers in their native
80-bit slots across replies (#44). StoreF32 from such a slot keeps upstream's
narrowing (the 80-bit value converted with round-to-odd) and then applies the
guest-RC store rounding of this patch, so the value is still rounded once.
The GPR pressure estimate for that StoreF32 case is unchanged at 3.

